/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  species/HTart.dat -- CASE-LOCAL model species for ed08 (the KHT model wine).

  Body fields (name, formula, charge, MW, hfAq) are data/standards/species/
  HTart.dat's, verbatim; the transport block is this case's addition, and it
  is the reason this file exists: the stack's conductivity, solution
  resistance and predicted limiting current all need a D0 for every ion, and
  the catalogue record carries none.

  THE TRANSPORT BLOCK IS AN ESTIMATE AND SAYS SO.  No limiting ionic
  conductivity of this anion was READ from a primary source when this record
  was written; a value written from memory would be an invisible falsehood.
  So the D0 is DERIVED by a stated method from facts the tree already
  carries, marked `origin estimated; reviewStatus unverified;`, and the
  engine ANNOUNCES it on every run that reads it.  Promotion to
  data/standards/ is a curation act reserved to a human review against a
  measured limiting conductance.

  The derivation (first written for the parked wine work-in-progress on
  2026-09-19, re-checked by hand here):
      V(KHT crystal)  = MW / rho_p = 188.177 / 1.980 = 95.04 cm3/mol
                        (data/standards/components/potassiumBitartrate.dat)
      V(HTart-)       = 95.04 - V0(K+) = 95.04 - 9.02 = 86.02 cm3/mol
                        (V0(K+): data/standards/species/K.dat, Millero 1971)
      r               = (3 V / (4 pi N_A))^(1/3) = 3.243e-10 m
      D0              = k T / (6 pi mu r) at 298.15 K, mu = 8.8987e-4 Pa s
                        (the Vogel fit of data/standards/components/water.dat)
                      = 7.568e-10 m2/s   (lambda0 = 28.4 S cm2/mol)
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType modelSpecies;
schemaVersion 1;

name    HTart;
formula     "HC4H4O6-";
charge  -1;
MW { value 149.08; unit g/mol;}
aqueousThermo
{
hfAq { value -1292100; unit J/mol;}
}

transport
{
    D0 { value 7.568e-10; unit m2/s; }
    provenance
    {
        source  "no primary read; derived from the tree's own records as stated in `method`";
        licence "this record's derivation is the project's own; the inputs are the catalogue's, cited in their own records";
        D0
        {
            origin        estimated;
            method        "Stokes-Einstein at 298.15 K on the engine's own water viscosity, 8.8987e-4 Pa s (the Vogel fit of data/standards/components/water.dat), with the anion radius taken as the sphere of ITS CRYSTAL MOLAR VOLUME: potassium bitartrate's rho_p = 1980 kg/m3 and MW 188.177 (data/standards/components/potassiumBitartrate.dat) give 95.04 cm3/mol per formula unit, minus K+'s conventional V0 = 9.02 cm3/mol (species/K.dat) = 86.02 cm3/mol for HC4H4O6-, r = 3.243e-10 m, D0 = 7.568e-10 m2/s (lambda0 = 28.4 S cm2/mol by Nernst-Einstein)";
            reviewStatus  unverified;
            notes         "Verify against a MEASURED limiting equivalent conductance of the hydrogen tartrate ion at 25 C from the electrolyte-conductance literature; none was read when this record was written, so none is cited. A crystal-volume Stokes radius is a lower bound on the hydrodynamic radius, so this D0 is more likely high than low.";
        }
    }
}
